course: dictionary
lesson: 11967
title: Categorical: certain, or sorted into groups
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Categorical has two meanings, and the sentence around it tells you which one to use. Most often it means very clear and certain, with no doubt at all. «Mum gave a categorical no when I asked for a puppy.» There was no maybe in her answer, so categorical means firm and final.
explain: Look for clues. Words like no, denial or never often show that the meaning is about certainty. Try your meaning in the sentence: «The coach made a categorical denial» becomes «The coach firmly said it was not true», and it still makes sense.
explain: In maths, categorical means to do with categories, or groups. «Our class sorted pets into groups: cats, dogs and fish.» Pets are not measured with numbers. They are sorted into groups, so this is categorical data. The clue is that the words describe groups, not amounts.
explain: The adverb is categorically, as in «The referee categorically denied the goal was offside.» It means in a clear, certain way, with no doubt at all.
fact: Categorical means very clear and certain, with no doubt at all.
fact: «A categorical no» means a firm, final no, not a maybe.
fact: In maths, categorical data is sorted into groups, such as favourite colour or type of pet.
fact: Categorical data is shown in tally charts, bar charts and pie charts.
fact: The adverb «categorically» means in a clear, certain way.
exercise: «Mum gave a categorical no when I asked for a puppy.» What does categorical mean here? | Quiet and gentle | *Very clear, with no doubt at all | Sorted into groups | Maybe, we will talk about it later
exercise: «The referee categorically denied the goal was offside.» What does categorically mean? | *In a clear, certain way with no doubt | By counting numbers carefully | In a slow and careful way | Very quickly
exercise: Favourite colour and type of pet are examples of which kind of data? | Data with decimal points | Data about the weather in numbers | *Categorical data, sorted into groups | Data measured in centimetres
test:
«Her answer was categorical: she would not come.» What does this tell the reader? | She might change her mind later | She is very tired | *There is no maybe, the answer is firm | She is shouting
test:
A bar chart of favourite fruits shows what kind of data? | Continuous data measured in metres | *Categorical data | Random numbers with no groups | Data that has no labels
exam:
Which word in «The teacher said categorically there would be no homework» shows she was completely certain? | said | teacher | *categorically | homework
